% ============================================================================
% hurricane-category-home-damage.query.adj — a worked recall over the
% meteorology hurricane-category-home-damage library.
% ============================================================================
% The shape a learner (or an LLM) produces to ANSWER "what does a category 3
% hurricane actually do to a well-built home?": it states no meteorology fact
% of its own — it only `import`s the grounded table and asks binding
% queries. The engine resolves each `?` against the `hurricane_home_damage`
% rows and returns the structural effect + the table's source/locator/trust.
%
% The last query runs the relation BACKWARD (bind the effect
% `total_roof_failure_and_wall_collapse`, recall which category it names —
% category_5). Unlike the parent `damage_level` table's summary word (which
% honestly duplicates `catastrophic_damage` for categories 4 and 5), this
% table's five structural effects are all distinct. A key that is not one of
% the five Saffir-Simpson categories abstains honestly — the engine never
% invents a damage effect.
%
% Run (from this directory so the relative import resolves):
%     ../../../../packages/rust/target/debug/adj-lang-cli hurricane-category-home-damage.query.adj
% ============================================================================

import "hurricane-category-home-damage.adj"

? hurricane_home_damage(category_1, $HomeDamageEffect)  % expect damage_to_roof_shingles_vinyl_siding_and_gutters
? hurricane_home_damage(category_5, $HomeDamageEffect)  % expect total_roof_failure_and_wall_collapse
? hurricane_home_damage($Category, total_roof_failure_and_wall_collapse)  % reverse bind — expect category_5
? hurricane_home_damage(category_6, $HomeDamageEffect)  % expect abstain — there is no category 6 on the scale
